STATUSES = [(295, "\n\n**295. Nothing makes a map of a level.** The guide's sections 7 and 8: a simplified automap mesh from the same layout -- room and corridor footprints, stairs, ramps, bridges, openings and landmarks kept; props, ceilings and tall walls left out; adjacent regions merged but never across a wall; holes and narrow passages kept; a shallow thickness and highlighted boundaries rather than a wireframe -- shown in a world of its own (`SubViewport`, `own_world_3d`) through an orthographic camera, with each marker placed by its storey and space and riding any display offset. Nothing in any repository makes a minimap, an automap or a map scene (read again). The interactive map is the game's -- the gameplay layer lives outside Level Factory -- but the mesh and the identities it needs are the factory's to make, from specs it already has. **What would close it:** a level's export carrying a map mesh per storey and the room and storey identity of every region, made from the specs, checked against roadmap 294's graph, and against the walk navmesh for traversal only (the guide: a navmesh's edges are an agent's centre, shrunk by its radius, not the building's); the guide's acceptance checks; the in-game map scene left to the game, with a reference scene to show the walker.", '*STATUS: OPEN 2026-10-22 -- the walker, seeing Deli Counter 0.258.0\'s scout view (296): "its ok for now, but i want to make it look more polished and artistic so it could ship to players....They can scout out the buildings and rotate the schemas in a 360 degree way". This item now carries that: a scout MODEL of each building -- the scout view\'s signals (walls cut low, doors and windows, stairs as treads, ladders, holes; storeys lifted apart; no furniture or labels) as a mesh a player turns all the way round -- shipped in each building\'s package and named by `buildings.json`, with a reference viewer scene (its own world, an orbit camera, storeys apart and together) to show the walker; judged as art, by frames the walker picks a style from, and priced as runtime content before it ships (one material, colour in vertex data; draws and frame time at the viewer). The in-game screen stays the game\'s. Not started. Earlier status kept: OPEN 2026-10-22 -- the walker, on 296\'s drawings: "assume that in game, a player could rotate it", and what players should see is "simpler but carry all of the signals needed" (`docs/findings/walker_review_2026-10-21_drawings/verdicts_2026-10-22/`). A view a player turns is a model rather than one drawing, and this item\'s map mesh -- footprints, stairs, openings and holes kept; props, ceilings and tall walls left out -- is that shape; 296 points here for it. Not started. Earlier status kept: OPEN 2026-10-21 -- the walker\'s floor-plan guide (`docs/reference/Floor_Plan_to_3D_Layout_and_Automap_Guide.md`), chased in `docs/findings/guides_2026-10-21/floor_plan.md`; not started.*', '*STATUS: NARROWED 2026-10-22 -- the scout models and the scout window ship in every package, and the gameplay layer calls the window through one stated seam. Deli Counter 0.259.0 (b97b1125, `patches/patch_dc_scout_model.py`): `scout_model.build_glb` builds the scout view\'s signals as a model -- slabs with their holes cut and rimmed, walls to the cut, windows as glass, doors as strips with entries their own colour, stairs as their treads, ladders -- a mesh a storey at its true elevation, one material, colour in COLOR_0; the model and the scout drawing agree on every door and window of five buildings (the agreement test caught a hole\'s rim wearing the entry\'s red); `package_drawings` writes `drawings/scout.glb`, kept. Deli Counter 0.260.0 (eab0ac3a, `patches/patch_dc_scout_points.py`): the walker\'s handheld palette, `palm`, the default ("roughly be in the style of a 1990s palm pilot...but with a little more backlight and color"), and the score and the props of interest ("buildings should show the \'score\' location and key props of interest"): a beacon at every objective marker, a lit block at every loot and camera marker, safe and ATM, a mesh of their own under each storey, `extras.points` naming each -- 131 scores, 139 cameras, 131 safes, 69 ATMs, 13 loot across the library. Level Factory 0.234.0 (c458dc2) names each model in `buildings.json`; cold run 9380 then STOPPED at the export, its import check waiting for the kept model -- Level Factory 0.234.1 (5c9d3d7, `patches/patch_lf_keep_model.py`) skips a model its sidecar keeps. Level Factory 0.235.0 (c61e134, `patches/patch_lf_scout_window.py`): every package carries `scout/scout_window.gd`, class `LFScoutWindow` -- open, close, is_open, buildings, select, get_screen_texture for the game\'s own handheld, set_points for gameplay\'s own score, turn, set_spread; signals opened, closed, building_selected -- and `scout/README.md` stating that seam ("make sure the window can be called by something on the gameplay layer (seam is clear and visible)"); the walk copy\'s overlay calls it on F6. Cold run 9381 (0 interventions; the level 9379\'s, eight files more): every model the bytes Deli Counter builds, none imported; in game, F6 opens the window over the level (`patches/lf_scout_window/ingame_*.png`), 319 draws walking, 348-388 open. LEFT: the walker\'s eye on it in game; a 239 ms hitch on the first open (the model loaded and the window\'s shaders compiled on first use) to warm or hide; the walk copy\'s caller to hold the player while the window is open (the arrow keys still move them); pixel type, a CC0 font download that is the walker\'s yes; the game\'s handheld asset and screen, the game\'s. Earlier status kept: OPEN 2026-10-22 -- the walker, seeing Deli Counter 0.258.0\'s scout view (296): "its ok for now, but i want to make it look more polished and artistic so it could ship to players....They can scout out the buildings and rotate the schemas in a 360 degree way". This item now carries that: a scout MODEL of each building -- the scout view\'s signals (walls cut low, doors and windows, stairs as treads, ladders, holes; storeys lifted apart; no furniture or labels) as a mesh a player turns all the way round -- shipped in each building\'s package and named by `buildings.json`, with a reference viewer scene (its own world, an orbit camera, storeys apart and together) to show the walker; judged as art, by frames the walker picks a style from, and priced as runtime content before it ships (one material, colour in vertex data; draws and frame time at the viewer). The in-game screen stays the game\'s. Not started. Earlier status kept: OPEN 2026-10-22 -- the walker, on 296\'s drawings: "assume that in game, a player could rotate it", and what players should see is "simpler but carry all of the signals needed" (`docs/findings/walker_review_2026-10-21_drawings/verdicts_2026-10-22/`). A view a player turns is a model rather than one drawing, and this item\'s map mesh -- footprints, stairs, openings and holes kept; props, ceilings and tall walls left out -- is that shape; 296 points here for it. Not started. Earlier status kept: OPEN 2026-10-21 -- the walker\'s floor-plan guide (`docs/reference/Floor_Plan_to_3D_Layout_and_Automap_Guide.md`), chased in `docs/findings/guides_2026-10-21/floor_plan.md`; not started.*')]


def main():
    raw = RM.read_bytes()
    if b"\r\n" in raw:
        raise SystemExit("REFUSED: the roadmap is LF and CRLF was found; nothing written")
    text = raw.decode("utf-8")
    if STATUSES[0][3] in text:
        raise SystemExit("REFUSED: already applied; nothing written")
    for n, heading, old, new in STATUSES:
        if text.count(heading) != 1:
            raise SystemExit("REFUSED: %d's heading is not there once; nothing written" % n)
        h = text.find(heading)
        s = max(text.rindex("\n\n", 0, h) + 2, text.rindex("\n*STATUS:", 0, h) + 1)
        if text[s:h] != old:
            raise SystemExit("REFUSED: %d's status block is not the one this was written against; nothing written" % n)
        text = text[:s] + new + text[h:]
        if text.count(new + heading) != 1:
            raise SystemExit("REFUSED: %d's status no longer adjoins its heading; nothing written" % n)
    RM.write_bytes(text.encode("utf-8"))
    print("roadmap: %d statuses replaced; now roadmap_status.py --write / --check" % len(STATUSES))
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
